1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:05,500
This is git-flare: a merge fabric for many coding agents working on one repository.

2
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:11,760
The platform runs entirely on Cloudflare: Workers, Durable Objects, Artifacts,

3
00:00:11,760 --> 00:00:14,184
Containers, Queues, and Workers AI.

4
00:00:15,134 --> 00:00:20,584
Point a swarm of coding agents at one Git repository, and three things break.

5
00:00:20,584 --> 00:00:25,424
Coordination: agents race for the same work, and collide on every push to main.

6
00:00:25,424 --> 00:00:30,564
Review: ten copies of the same model agreeing are not ten independent opinions.

7
00:00:30,564 --> 00:00:35,184
And trust: on Artifacts, as on plain Git, any write token can push to main,

8
00:00:35,184 --> 00:00:36,344
or rewrite it.

9
00:00:36,344 --> 00:00:40,244
Every measurement in this video comes from a script in the repository.

10
00:00:41,282 --> 00:00:42,872
Here is the shape of it.

11
00:00:42,872 --> 00:00:47,512
Agents use a small command-line tool, G F, and plain git.

12
00:00:47,512 --> 00:00:52,652
Tasks live in sharded Durable Objects, which lease each task to exactly one agent,

13
00:00:52,652 --> 00:00:54,392
with a fencing epoch.

14
00:00:54,392 --> 00:00:58,952
Each agent pushes to its own Artifacts fork, then submits the commit.

15
00:00:58,952 --> 00:01:04,792
The platform computes the diff itself, from repository objects, and runs its gates.

16
00:01:04,792 --> 00:01:07,912
Reviewers from different model families score that diff.

17
00:01:07,912 --> 00:01:12,152
Approved patches reach a single merge queue, the only writer of main.

18
00:01:12,152 --> 00:01:15,292
It composes compatible patches into one commit,

19
00:01:15,292 --> 00:01:19,652
runs the project's own tests in a Container with no internet,

20
00:01:19,652 --> 00:01:22,112
and pushes with a compare-and-swap.

21
00:01:22,112 --> 00:01:24,672
Read replicas spread the clones, and a guard,

22
00:01:24,672 --> 00:01:28,008
fed by Artifacts events through Queues, watches main.

23
00:01:28,958 --> 00:01:31,348
Let's run it, live on Cloudflare.

24
00:01:31,348 --> 00:01:36,648
One command starts five coding agents, running Qwen Coder on Workers AI,

25
00:01:36,648 --> 00:01:39,388
two rogue agents, and ten scripted workers,

26
00:01:39,388 --> 00:01:43,572
on a fresh repository with four task shards and two replicas.

27
00:01:44,522 --> 00:01:48,892
Each agent joins, gets its own fork, and claims one task.

28
00:01:48,892 --> 00:01:52,692
The models write the code and its tests, run the tests locally,

29
00:01:52,692 --> 00:01:53,992
and submit the commit.

30
00:01:53,992 --> 00:01:58,056
On the dashboard, claims turn into patches, waiting for review.

31
00:01:59,006 --> 00:02:02,616
Two agents are hostile. One breaks the add function.

32
00:02:02,616 --> 00:02:06,676
The other breaks it too, and edits the test so that it still passes.

33
00:02:06,676 --> 00:02:09,856
The diff comes from the platform, not from the agent,

34
00:02:09,856 --> 00:02:12,696
so the reviewers see both changes.

35
00:02:12,696 --> 00:02:16,156
Llama and Mistral reject them, and Mistral spells it out:

36
00:02:16,156 --> 00:02:19,616
the tests were altered to pass the incorrect implementation.

37
00:02:20,642 --> 00:02:24,472
Every LLM patch was approved by at least two model families.

38
00:02:24,472 --> 00:02:29,132
On one of them, Mistral said no, and Llama and OpenAI outweighed it:

39
00:02:29,132 --> 00:02:33,712
evidence adds up across independent families, while inside one family,

40
00:02:33,712 --> 00:02:37,080
each extra opinion counts half as much as the one before.

41
00:02:38,030 --> 00:02:40,620
Approved patches go to the merge queue.

42
00:02:40,620 --> 00:02:44,880
Fifteen patches landed in four commits, the largest batching eight,

43
00:02:44,880 --> 00:02:49,020
and before every push, the project's tests ran in the container.

44
00:02:49,020 --> 00:02:52,420
Main moved four times, each time with a compare-and-swap,

45
00:02:52,420 --> 00:02:54,324
and both replicas followed.

46
00:02:55,274 --> 00:02:59,924
The status agrees: the guard has seen no write from outside the merge queue,

47
00:02:59,924 --> 00:03:01,920
and both replicas are current.

48
00:03:02,870 --> 00:03:08,720
Now, trust. Artifacts has no branch protection: any write token can move main.

49
00:03:08,720 --> 00:03:10,400
So let's play the attacker.

50
00:03:10,400 --> 00:03:14,100
With account access, we mint a write token outside git-flare,

51
00:03:14,100 --> 00:03:16,820
and push an innocent-looking commit to main.

52
00:03:16,820 --> 00:03:20,556
It adds an install script that pipes a download into a shell.

53
00:03:21,506 --> 00:03:25,016
Artifacts reports both, the new token and the push,

54
00:03:25,016 --> 00:03:26,996
as events delivered through Queues.

55
00:03:26,996 --> 00:03:30,796
Seconds later, the guard flags a write token that git-flare never minted,

56
00:03:30,796 --> 00:03:33,984
and stops every merge until an operator decides.

57
00:03:34,934 --> 00:03:39,424
One command puts main back to the last head the merge queue produced,

58
00:03:39,424 --> 00:03:43,104
revokes every write token on the repository, and merges resume.

59
00:03:43,104 --> 00:03:48,060
The replicas never saw the bad commit: while an alert is up, they are not synced.

60
00:03:49,010 --> 00:03:53,740
The project's tests run in a Cloudflare Container started with internet disabled.

61
00:03:53,740 --> 00:03:57,300
Dependencies come from a read-only proxy to the npm registry,

62
00:03:57,300 --> 00:03:59,920
and the tests run as an unprivileged user.

63
00:03:59,920 --> 00:04:04,020
A probe test, submitted as an ordinary patch, found no internet,

64
00:04:04,020 --> 00:04:08,560
a proxy that refuses writes, and installed packages it could not touch.

65
00:04:08,560 --> 00:04:13,060
Run as root, the same probe rewrote a dependency, broke another test,

66
00:04:13,060 --> 00:04:13,860
and was rejected.

67
00:04:13,860 --> 00:04:18,860
And nothing of a run survives into the next: a test that left a process running,

68
00:04:18,860 --> 00:04:22,100
and files behind, found neither one run later.

69
00:04:22,100 --> 00:04:25,416
With that cleanup broken on purpose, the check failed.

70
00:04:26,366 --> 00:04:29,296
Now scale, measured on Cloudflare.

71
00:04:29,296 --> 00:04:31,876
Under load, we saw no rate-limit errors from Artifacts:

72
00:04:31,876 --> 00:04:35,796
past a few hundred operations per second, its control plane queues,

73
00:04:35,796 --> 00:04:36,996
and responses slow down.

74
00:04:36,996 --> 00:04:40,196
Our first claim path called Artifacts three times per claim,

75
00:04:40,196 --> 00:04:41,696
and twice more at submit,

76
00:04:41,696 --> 00:04:46,096
and topped out at a hundred and twenty-one claims per second.

77
00:04:46,096 --> 00:04:48,576
So claims stopped calling Artifacts.

78
00:04:48,576 --> 00:04:50,556
On a run of the same size,

79
00:04:50,556 --> 00:04:52,856
the platform went from a hundred and twenty-one to

80
00:04:52,856 --> 00:04:56,316
almost seven hundred claims per second,

81
00:04:56,316 --> 00:05:00,204
and a longer run passed a thousand, from a single test client.

82
00:05:01,154 --> 00:05:02,684
The coordination layer stays flat.

83
00:05:02,684 --> 00:05:06,284
These numbers come from a bench Worker calling the Durable Objects directly,

84
00:05:06,284 --> 00:05:07,724
with Artifacts mocked.

85
00:05:07,724 --> 00:05:11,104
A single task shard handles about a thousand claims per second,

86
00:05:11,104 --> 00:05:16,704
and thirty-two shards reached thirteen and nineteen thousand, in two of three runs.

87
00:05:16,704 --> 00:05:20,284
Recording a review used to slow down as the approval graph grew,

88
00:05:20,284 --> 00:05:23,624
down to sixty-five per second at twenty thousand edges.

89
00:05:23,624 --> 00:05:28,044
Now it holds around nine hundred to fourteen hundred per second,

90
00:05:28,044 --> 00:05:30,432
up to a hundred thousand edges.

91
00:05:31,382 --> 00:05:36,152
And when hundreds of agents clone at once, a single repository starts failing.

92
00:05:36,152 --> 00:05:40,892
With four replicas serving the clones, kept in sync by the merge queue,

93
00:05:40,892 --> 00:05:43,992
failed clones dropped from about a quarter to five percent,

94
00:05:43,992 --> 00:05:47,340
and successful clones per second almost tripled.

95
00:05:48,290 --> 00:05:50,860
What isn't solved yet, we say plainly.

96
00:05:50,860 --> 00:05:52,940
Conflicts are detected per file.

97
00:05:52,940 --> 00:05:55,420
Test dependencies are npm only.

98
00:05:55,420 --> 00:06:00,220
One registry per repository records about a thousand reviews per second.

99
00:06:00,220 --> 00:06:03,940
Onboarding is bounded by fork creation, a few seconds per agent.

100
00:06:03,940 --> 00:06:06,380
And near-duplicate detection is textual,

101
00:06:06,380 --> 00:06:10,536
so similar patches are flagged for reviewers, never silently closed.

102
00:06:11,486 --> 00:06:17,856
From zero to a submitted patch takes five commands: log in, join, claim, commit,

103
00:06:17,856 --> 00:06:18,756
and submit.

104
00:06:18,756 --> 00:06:23,216
The platform deploys with wrangler, and runs locally with no Cloudflare account,

105
00:06:23,216 --> 00:06:25,500
with Artifacts and the tests mocked.

106
00:06:26,450 --> 00:06:29,300
git-flare. Many agents, one main,

107
00:06:29,300 --> 00:06:32,140
and nothing merged that the platform did not check.

108
00:06:32,140 --> 00:06:36,528
The code, the scripts, and every raw result are in the repository.
